orithms for semipaired domination in graphs. *Combinatorial Algorithms*, 278–289. *Lecture Notes in Comput. Sci.*, **11638**, Springer, Cham. 2019.\n\n## 2020\n17. T. W. Haynes, S. T. Hedetniemi, and M. A. Henning, Glossary of common terms. *Topics in Domination in Graphs*, 1–11. *Developments in Mathematics* **64** Springer, Cham. 2020.  \n18. T. W. Haynes, S. T. Hedetniemi, and M. A. Henning, Models of domination in graphs. *Topics in Domination in Graphs*, 13–30. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nModels of Domination in Graphs | Request PDF (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/346307392_Models_of_Domination_in_Graphs)\nciteturn2search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6.1 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago;   * October 2020 ... Teresa W Haynes ... Henning ... Image: Request Full-text Paper PDF ... It is known in the literature that for any spanning tree T of a graph G, [9,18,20,22] Recently, a new technique in the establishment of sharp bounds on the radius and diameter in graphs has been presented in [22] by employing Fact 1 together with the Danklemann-Entringer [6] method. ... The study of domination in graphs is an on going process, see for instance recent research in [10,11,19,22]. ...\n\n# Models of Domination in Graphs\n\n  * October 2020\n  * Developments in Mathematics\n\nDOI:10.1007/978-3-030-51117-3_2\n\n  * In book: Topics in Domination in Graphs (pp.13-30)\n\nAuthors:\n\nTeresa W Haynes\n\n  * East Tennessee State University\n\nStephen T. Hedetniemi\n\n  * Clemson University\n\nMichael A. Henning\n\n  * University of Johannesburg\n\n\n## Abstract\n\nA set S of vertices in a graph G is a dominating set if every vertex not in S is adjacent to at least one vertex in S. In this chapter, we present logical models called frameworks, each of which gives a different perspective of dominating sets.\n\nImage: ResearchGate Logo\n\nDiscover the world's research\n\n  * 25+ million members\n  * 160+ million publication pages\n  * 2.3+ billion citations\nJoin for free\n\n## No full-text available\n\nImage: Request Full-text Paper PDF\n\nTo read the full-text of this research,\nyou can request a copy directly from the authors.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nChartrand, VanderJagt and Yue defined and studied two invariants on undirected graphs that are based on the domination number of the orientations of the graph [5]. The concept presented in [5], which is the main topic of this paper, is defined as follows. Given an undirected graph \\(G\\), the *orientable domination number* of \\(G\\) is (https://users.fmf.uni-lj.si/klavzar/preprints/2211.02395.pdf)\nciteturn2search15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4.0 years ago; Chartrand, VanderJagt and Yue defined and studied two invariants on undirected graphs that are based on the domination number of the orientations of the graph [5]. ... With this paper we initiate the study of orientable domination in graph products and with respect to related graph operations.\nChartrand, VanderJagt and Yue defined and studied two invariants on undirected graphs that are based on the domination number of the orientations of the graph [5]. The concept presented in [5], which is the main topic of this paper, is defined as follows. Given an undirected graph \\(G\\), the *orientable domination number* of \\(G\\) is\n\n\\[\n\\mathrm{DOM}(G)=\\max\\{\\gamma(D): D \\text{ is an orientation of } G\\}.\n\\]\n\nReplacing max with min in the above definition gives the (ordinary) domination number \\(\\gamma(G)\\) of \\(G\\). The first result on orientable domination number, although stated in a different language, was proved by Erdős [7] who found the following bounds for \\(\\mathrm{DOM}(K_n)\\), where \\(n \\ge 2\\): \\(\\log_2 n - 2\\log_2(\\log_2 n) \\le \\mathrm{DOM}(K_n) \\le \\log_2(n+1)\\). Interestingly, the exact values of the orientable domination number of complete graphs are still not known. Szekeres and Szekeres [17] improved the upper bound of Erdős to arrive at the following result:\n\n\\[\n\\log_2 n - 2\\log_2(\\log_2 n) \\le \\mathrm{DOM}(K_n) \\le \\log_2 n - \\log_2(\\log_2 n) + 2. \\qquad (1)\n\\]\n\nLu, Wang and Wong [12] gave a short proof of the above upper bound. Dominating sets in tournaments were studied in [15] and used in [1].\n\nOne of the main open problems in domination in graphs is Vizing’s conjecture from [18], which considers domination in the Cartesian product of graphs (see the survey paper [2] and the references therein). A number of papers are devoted not only to the conjecture itself, but also to several other domination invariants in various graph products. With this paper we initiate the study of orientable domination in graph products and with respect to related graph operations.\n\nIn the next section, we establish notation and mention some preliminary results needed throughout the paper. In Section 3, we determine the orientable domination number of the corona \\(G \\circ H\\) of arbitrary graphs \\(G\\) and \\(H\\), which is expressed as a function of the orientable domination numbers of \\(G\\) and \\(H\\). In Section 4, we prove a sharp lower and upper bound on the orientable domination number of the Cartesian product of two graphs \\(G\\) and \\(H\\). --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDiscussiones Mathematicae (https://bibliotekanauki.pl/articles/31348166.pdf)\nciteturn2search16 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.6 years ago; Graph Theory 40 (2020) 995–1004 ... domination in digraphs, Bull. ... For notation and graph theory terminology, we in general follow Haynes, Hedet-\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDominator colorings of digraphs - PISRT (https://pisrt.org/psr-press/journals/odam/04-vol-3-2020-issue-2/dominator-colorings-of-digraphs/)\nciteturn2search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6.2 years ago; Crawled: last month;   * Volume 3